VALUES (
'2026/05/29/hdq-month-may-2026',
'month', 'month',
'The Month the TSX Quietly Changed What It Was Betting On',
'Oil fell sixteen percent and a war stayed open, yet Canadian equities closed May at record highs. The months data reveals an index that quietly relocated its risk from the oil price to the rate path.',
'<ul><li><strong>The TSX rose 1.4% to a record while oil fell 16%,</strong><span> closing the month at 34,518 after an intraday all-time high of 34,575 on May 22, even as WTI crude collapsed from its May 19 peak.</span></li><li><strong>The indexs correlation to crude inverted,</strong><span> from roughly zero in early May to minus 0.87 in the second half, the signature of a market rotating out of energy and into rate-sensitive sectors.</span></li><li><strong>Gold fell 5% through an open war,</strong><span> because rising real yields, not geopolitics, governed the gold price while oil held its supply bid.</span></li><li><strong>The five-year yield tracked oil, not inflation,</strong><span> ignoring a benign 2.8% April CPI and finishing 20 basis points below its peak as crude broke down.</span></li><li><strong>Canada confirmed a technical recession on the final day,</strong><span> with Q1 GDP at minus 0.1%, reframing the rate-relief rotation as also a recession-fear rotation heading into June.</span></li></ul>',
'<style>.mia-lede{font-size:18px;line-height:1.6;color:#15233b}.mia-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:13px;margin:8px 0 14px}@media(max-width:680px){.mia-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}}.mia-stat{background:#fff;border:1px solid #e4e7ec;border-top:3px solid #1a3560;padding:15px 15px 13px;border-radius:3px}.mia-stat.g{border-top-color:#e8a825}.mia-stat.u{border-top-color:#3a7a55}.mia-stat.d{border-top-color:#8a3030}.mia-stat .v{font-family:"Bricolage Grotesque",sans-serif;font-weight:800;font-size:27px;line-height:1;color:#15233b;letter-spacing:-.02em}.mia-stat .v small{font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:#8b93a0}.mia-stat .l{font-size:11px;color:#6b7280;margin-top:8px;line-height:1.35;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em;font-weight:600}.mia-stat .c{font-size:12px;font-weight:700;margin-top:6px}.mia-stat .c.p{color:#3a7a55}.mia-stat .c.n{color:#8a3030}.mia-stat .c.f{color:#7e93b4}.mia-pull{border-left:3px solid #e8a825;padding:6px 0 6px 22px;margin:30px 0;font-family:"Bricolage Grotesque",sans-serif;font-weight:600;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32;color:#1a3560}.mia-scen{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:14px;margin:14px 0 6px}@media(max-width:760px){.mia-scen{grid-template-columns:1fr}}.mia-card{border:1px solid #e4e7ec;border-radius:4px;overflow:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.mia-card .t{padding:13px 15px;color:#fff}.mia-card.base .t{background:#1a3560}.mia-card.bull .t{background:#2c5f44}.mia-card.bear .t{background:#6e2a2a}.mia-card .t .nm{font-family:"Bricolage Grotesque",sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:16px}.mia-card .t .pr{font-size:11px;opacity:.82;margin-top:2px}.mia-card .bd{padding:13px 15px;font-size:13.5px;flex:1}.mia-card .bd p{margin-bottom:9px}.mia-card .tr{border-top:1px solid #e4e7ec;padding:11px 15px;background:#f7f8fa}.mia-card .tr .lb{font-size:9.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6b7280;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:6px}.mia-card .tr ul{list-style:none;font-size:12px;margin:0;padding:0}.mia-card .tr li{padding:3px 0;color:#15233b}.mia-card .tr li:before{content:"\25B8 ";color:#c98e16}.mia-tbl-w{overflow-x:auto;border:1px solid #e4e7ec;border-radius:4px;margin:14px 0}.mia-tbl{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:12.5px;min-width:600px}.mia-tbl th,.mia-tbl td{padding:8px 12px;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid #eef1f5;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.mia-tbl th{background:#1a3560;color:#fff;font-weight:600;font-size:11px}.mia-tbl th:first-child,.mia-tbl td:first-child{text-align:left}.mia-tbl td.na{color:#c8cdd5}</style>
<p class="mia-lede">Four numbers frame May. The index rose. Oil round-tripped. Gold fell. The five-year yield finished lower than it started. Each was unremarkable alone. Held together across seventeen trading sessions, they describe a Canadian market that migrated its entire risk thesis from one sector to another, in plain sight, while the headlines were still about a war, and then learned on the last day of the month that the economy had been in recession the whole time.</p>
<h2 id="glance" data-toc="The Month at a Glance" data-toc-n="01"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Section 01</span>The Month at a Glance</h2>
<div class="mia-grid"><div class="mia-stat u"><div class="v">34,518</div><div class="l">TSX close, May 29</div><div class="c p">+1.4% MTD, record 34,575</div></div><div class="mia-stat d"><div class="v"><small>US$</small>87.20</div><div class="l">WTI crude, May 29</div><div class="c n">-16.2% from May 19 peak</div></div><div class="mia-stat d"><div class="v"><small>US$</small>4,543</div><div class="l">Gold spot, May 29</div><div class="c n">-5% off early-May highs</div></div><div class="mia-stat g"><div class="v">-0.87</div><div class="l">TSX-to-oil corr, 2nd half</div><div class="c f">inverted from -0.08</div></div><div class="mia-stat "><div class="v">3.11<small>%</small></div><div class="l">GoC 5-year yield</div><div class="c n">-20 bps from May 20 peak</div></div><div class="mia-stat "><div class="v">2.25<small>%</small></div><div class="l">Bank of Canada rate</div><div class="c f">held; June decision ahead</div></div><div class="mia-stat d"><div class="v">-0.1<small>%</small></div><div class="l">Q1 GDP, annualised</div><div class="c n">first recession since 2020</div></div><div class="mia-stat "><div class="v">2.8<small>%</small></div><div class="l">Canada CPI, April</div><div class="c f">benign; bond market ignored it</div></div></div>
<p>The dashboard reads as eight independent facts. The rest of this report is the argument that they are one fact, observed from eight angles: the index relocated its sensitivity from the oil price to the rate path, and by month-end the rate path was being driven by a recession nobody had confirmed until the close.</p>
<h2 id="arc" data-toc="The Arc of the Month" data-toc-n="02"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Section 02</span>The Arc of the Month</h2>
<p>May opened with the market still trading the war. The Strait of Hormuz had been disrupted since early March, Brent sat above US$100, and the TSX behaved like a leveraged claim on the oil price: energy names carried the index, financials lagged, and every Iran headline moved the tape.</p>
<p>It did not end there. By the third week, crude had reversed its entire war premium, the energy trade had drained, and the index was making record highs on the strength of the sectors that benefit when oil and yields fall. The chart below traces that handover. The shaded band marks the stretch where the index pulled away from crude and never looked back.</p>
